 ## Background
-Radiance-field optimization emerged prominently with the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020/2021, which popularized the use of implicit neural representations for novel view synthesis [S2][S3, S4, unofficial]. Prior to this, methods like Local Light Field Fusion (LLFF) used explicit multiplane images for forward-facing scenes [S5]. The field has since expanded to include explicit grid-based methods like Plenoxels [S1, S3, unofficial] and TensoRF [S1, S3, unofficial], as well as non-MLP representations like 3D Gaussian Splatting [S3, S4, unofficial]. The underlying principle relies on the discrete Volume Rendering Equation (VRE), where radiance is calculated by a single-path summation along the ray trajectory, avoiding the need for full path tracing [S2]. This approach has been applied to various domains including dynamic scenes (Neural Volumes) [S2], medical imaging (EndoNeRF) [S7], and non-line-of-sight imaging (NeTF) [S9, unofficial].
+Radiance-field optimization emerged as a dominant paradigm in computer graphics and vision for novel view synthesis and inverse rendering. The foundational work on Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. (2021) demonstrated the efficacy of learning continuous volumetric representations using MLPs and differentiable volume rendering [S2][S3, S4, unofficial]. Prior to this, methods like Neural Volumes (Mildenhall et al., 2018) explored explicit voxel grids with learned warp fields for dynamic scenes [S1, unofficial][S2]. The field has since expanded to include explicit representations like 3D Gaussian Splatting (Kerbl et al., 2023) and Plenoxels, as well as specialized applications in medical imaging (EndoNeRF) and non-line-of-sight imaging (NeTF) [S2][S6, unofficial][S7]. The core insight is that the volume rendering equation is differentiable with respect to scene parameters, allowing gradient-based optimization to reconstruct 3D scenes from 2D images [S2].
 
